.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.TH tapectl-collection-plan 1  "tapectl-collection-plan " 
.SH NAME
tapectl\-collection\-plan \- Show how each collection\*(Aqs pending units pack into tapes
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBtapectl collection plan\fR [\fB\-\-copies\fR] [\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-\-generation\fR] [\fB\-n\fR|\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR] [\fB\-\-device\fR] [\fB\-v\fR|\fB\-\-verbose\fR] [\fB\-\-fill\-ceiling\fR] [\fB\-q\fR|\fB\-\-quiet\fR] [\fB\-\-policy\-aware\fR] [\fB\-y\fR|\fB\-\-yes\fR] [\fB\-\-config\fR] [\fB\-\-home\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] 
.SH DESCRIPTION
Show how each collection\*(Aqs pending units pack into tapes
.PP
The units are packed in name order, each tape filled until the next unit does not fit (next\-fit): each batch is one tape\*(Aqs worth. A unit that allows spanning (`allow_spanning`) is not held to one tape: it goes last in its batch, and the plan names the unit that spans onto the next cartridge. It is refused when it is larger than the staging area, or, on a drive that does not declare `post_warning_bytes`, larger than the room under the fill ceiling.
.PP
The units already staged and still owed a copy are listed after the batches, with the command that writes them.
.PP
A plan needs no drive: it is sized for `\-\-generation`, or the configured drive\*(Aqs own generation, and never opens or probes the drive, which need not be attached. `volume init` decides each cartridge\*(Aqs real capacity, and `collection run` packs against it.
.PP
Exit status: 0 = clean; 1 = a unit refused; 2 = errors (a collection\*(Aqs root, tenant or archive set, or neither a drive configured nor `\-\-generation` given). Every collection is planned whatever another\*(Aqs problems.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-copies\fR \fI<COPIES>\fR [default: 2]
How many copies to count cartridges for (the batches stay the same)
.TP
\fB\-\-generation\fR \fI<GENERATION>\fR
Plan for tapes of this generation (LTO\-5, LTO\-6) instead of the drive\*(Aqs

E.g. sizing batches for LTO\-5 stock that an LTO\-6 drive will write. No cartridge need be loaded, and with no drive configured the plan takes a drive\*(Aqs defaults (the 97% fill ceiling, the 50M end\-of\-tape reserve).
.TP
\fB\-\-device\fR \fI<DEVICE>\fR
The configured drive to plan against, by its device path

Only needed when more than one `[[backends.lto]]` is configured.
.TP
\fB\-\-fill\-ceiling\fR \fI<FILL_CEILING>\fR
Fill each tape to at most this fraction: `0.99` or `99%`

For this command only. Overrides the drive\*(Aqs `fill_ceiling` (default 97%).
.TP
\fB\-\-policy\-aware\fR
Pack first the units a write would help (too few copies or places)

Those whose audit finds too few copies or a required location with no copy (a never\-archived unit always does). Each group stays in name order. Batch numbers then follow this order, so give `collection run` the same flag to run the batch reviewed here.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
.SH "GLOBAL OPTIONS"
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
Output in JSON format, errors included

Under it an error is one JSON document on stdout, `{"error": …, "exit_code": N}`, and the exit code is the command\*(Aqs own.
.TP
\fB\-n\fR, \fB\-\-dry\-run\fR
Show what would be done without making changes

Commands that cannot preview refuse the flag rather than ignore it.
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
Debug tracing on stderr

Raises the log level to DEBUG (never lowers a `[logging] level` set higher); it never changes what a command does or prints on stdout.
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR
No live progress on stderr

Long operations (stage create, volume write/resume/verify/read\-slices, restore) still write their session log under <home>/logs/.
.TP
\fB\-y\fR, \fB\-\-yes\fR
Answer yes to the confirmation prompts of risks you may accept

It never reaches a refusal: what is refused is a fact, not a risk to accept (ADR\-0008, Tier 3), and a command\*(Aqs own \-\-force names what it overrides.
.TP
\fB\-\-config\fR \fI<CONFIG>\fR
Path to config file

NOTE: on its own this ALSO relocates the whole tapectl home to the config file\*(Aqs parent directory — database, keys, staging, stage reports. That is how every test harness gets an isolated home, so it still works, but it is surprising enough that it now warns. Use \-\-home when you mean "operate on a different archive", and \-\-config only to point at a config file inside that home.
.TP
\fB\-\-home\fR \fI<HOME>\fR
tapectl home directory: database, keys, catalogs, stage\-reports, logs

The home is the first of: \-\-home; TAPECTL_HOME; the directory of \-\-config; this account\*(Aqs ~/.tapectl, when it exists; the home named by the home pointer /etc/tapectl/home (TAPECTL_HOME_POINTER names another file), when the account has no ~/.tapectl of its own; else ~/.tapectl, where `tapectl init` makes a new one. The config file is <home>/config.toml unless \-\-config names another. `tapectl status` and `tapectl config check` name the home in use and the rule that chose it.

As root the home pointer is never read, and on a host that has one tapectl refuses unless \-\-home or TAPECTL_HOME names the home: the service user\*(Aqs commands run through `tapectl\-op`.
